ory" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/state.json" "file_sha256" "15bb36e4a33528b15ce06585d4ffa0af26fc129784a4a37601d3ffb791778fce"} {"question" "q-333034" "field" "field-8" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\lambda }_{2}>0"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/state.json" "file_sha256" "15bb36e4a33528b15ce06585d4ffa0af26fc129784a4a37601d3ffb791778fce"} {"question" "q-333141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/1d2a232b4351c84871d89ba25384aa53777760355ceea6458b07fba9c0a9ed78.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/122759a459c8dd56dbe69c44c0da4ccffbbc660465fbedc7457e17b42430436e.png)\n\nFinally, since the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-9}},\n solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector \n{{field-10}} \nas they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/fa5033f974e501bb8bf6960753b1706569f5ed59c3dec6d22e8b200317f2a487.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/state.json" "file_sha256" "15bb36e4a33528b15ce06585d4ffa0af26fc129784a4a37601d3ffb791778fce"} {"question" "q-333141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/1d2a232b4351c84871d89ba25384aa53777760355ceea6458b07fba9c0a9ed78.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/122759a459c8dd56dbe69c44c0da4ccffbbc660465fbedc7457e17b42430436e.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0<{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}\n$$\n\nthus, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$  solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector  ${v}_{1}$ as they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/assets/fcaa3ba63a1e6ad061eecf1f72caf3c9f8ba78b014eff044ee5be682e1325128.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/state.json" "file_sha256" "15bb36e4a33528b15ce06585d4ffa0af26fc129784a4a37601d3ffb791778fce"} {"question" "q-333141" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072827/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ced7682c36eec5d984ea10c9d9522fdf027654980487c4f9